.SH CAVEATS
On macOS,
.B \-x
tells volumes apart by each volume's own device, not by the device number
the volumes of a boot volume group share, so
.I /System/Volumes/Data
counts as a volume of its own:
.B duscope \-x /System/Volumes
leaves it out, where BSD
.B du \-x
enters it.
The firmlinks of the system volume are entered, so
.B duscope \-x /
measures what
.B du \-x /
does.
.PP
On Linux, an automount point the kernel has not mounted yet (an NFS
submount, debugfs's
.IR tracing )
is listed and not entered, since opening it would mount a filesystem:
it is entered only with
.B \-\-include\-network
and without
.BR \-x .
.PP
.B \-\-exclude\-caches
looks for a cache rule's marker files only beside the directory, in its
parent:
.I node_modules
is left out next to a
.IR package.json ,
not when the
.I package.json
is further up the tree.
The
.B caches
verb, which comes with saved scans, looks for markers in every ancestor,
so it can report a cache the scan entered.
.PP
Patterns given to
.B \-\-exclude
and
.BR \-\-exclude\-from ,
and the list verbs'
.B \-\-glob
and
.BR \-\-exclude ,
match names case\-sensitively, also on a case\-insensitive volume (APFS
and HFS+ by default):
.B \-\-exclude \(aq*.MOV\(aq
does not leave out
.IR a.mov .
.PP
On macOS
.B rescan
reads again only what the volumes' FSEvents history reports changed since
the saved scan.
On the startup disk's Data volume that history does not record changes
made from recoveryOS or from another system between a shutdown and the
next startup, so a rescan after such changes can miss them.
The age limit
.RB ( \-\-max\-age ,
7 days by default) and the post\-check, which reads a sample of the
directories again before the new scan is saved, stand behind it;
.B duscope rescan \-\-full
reads the whole tree.
Reading a file changes nothing that history records: a rescan of a scan
taken with
.B \-\-attrs full
keeps the access times that scan read for whatever it takes from it.
So that they are never older than the age limit, a scan with
.B \-\-attrs full
that was itself an incremental rescan is read whole.
